-- title:
--   Ratios of integral q-expansions form a field
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, write $\Gamma'$ for its image as a subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$, and assume that $1$ lies in `strictPeriods` of $\Gamma'$. Let $x$ be a Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$. Call a power series $p \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ an integral $q$-expansion of a function $f$ on the upper half-plane when the image of $p$ under $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{C}$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f$ of width $1$, and write $\overline{p} \in \mathbb{Q}((q))$ for the Laurent series obtained from $p$ by casting its coefficients into $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $R_\Gamma \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$ be the set [`ModularCurve.intFormRatiosC ℚ Γ`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L83) of those elements expressible as $\overline{p_f}/\overline{p_g}$ for some weight $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, some modular forms $f, g$ of weight $k$ on $\Gamma'$ and some $p_f, p_g \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ that are integral $q$-expansions of $f$ and of $g$ respectively, with $\overline{p_g} \neq 0$. The assertion is that $x$ belongs to the intermediate field $\mathbb{Q}(R_\Gamma)$ of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by $R_\Gamma$ (the field [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ Γ`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101), defined as `IntermediateField.adjoin ℚ` applied to $R_\Gamma$) if and only if $x$ already belongs to $R_\Gamma$; that is, $R_\Gamma$ is itself a subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ containing $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   This identifies the $q$-expansion function field attached to $\Gamma$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ with the bare set of quotients of integral $q$-expansions of two modular forms of one and the same weight, so that no iterated field operations are needed to exhibit an element of it. It is used in the construction of models of $X_1(M)$ and of the associated Hecke and diamond operator data, where elements of the function field must be produced as single such quotients.
-- source:
